The `Segmentation fault` in `test_get_true` indicates a low-level memory error, most likely a null pointer dereference. The test involves inserting `MANY` (40) entries, which will likely trigger a resize operation within the hash map implementation.

A common pitfall when using `realloc` is to unconditionally assign its return value to the pointer being reallocated without checking if `realloc` succeeded. If `realloc` fails, it returns `NULL` and leaves the original memory block untouched. If the `NULL` return value is then assigned to the hash map's internal bucket pointer, any subsequent access to that pointer will result in a segmentation fault.

The `sys_hashmap` library is part of Zephyr's core, and its implementation is typically found in `lib/sys/hash_map.c`. The `test_get_true` test is merely exposing this underlying defect.

The fix involves modifying the `sys_hashmap_grow` (or equivalent resize) function to check the return value of the allocation function (which is `realloc` by default) immediately. If the allocation fails, the function should return an error, preserving the original, valid bucket array. The bucket pointer should only be updated if the allocation was successful.

Assuming a structure similar to the following in `lib/sys/hash_map.c`:

```c
